1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,872
How do you tell the world about a payment without ever losing the news, or inventing it?

2
00:00:04,922 --> 00:00:08,952
Picture an app that saves a payment as paid, and then tells the shop.

3
00:00:09,002 --> 00:00:12,336
If it crashes between those two steps, the shop never hears.

4
00:00:12,386 --> 00:00:15,648
The shop ships nothing, and the buyer has paid for nothing.

5
00:00:15,698 --> 00:00:18,048
Swap the order, and it is worse.

6
00:00:18,098 --> 00:00:21,672
The app tells the shop paid, then crashes before saving.

7
00:00:21,722 --> 00:00:24,912
Now the shop ships goods for a payment that does not exist.

8
00:00:24,962 --> 00:00:28,224
Two separate steps can always be split by a crash.

9
00:00:28,274 --> 00:00:30,480
Think of an office with an outbox tray.

10
00:00:30,530 --> 00:00:34,416
You close the file and drop the letter in the tray in one motion.

11
00:00:34,466 --> 00:00:37,152
Later, a postman collects from the tray.

12
00:00:37,202 --> 00:00:42,144
If you are called away, the letter is either in the tray or the file is still open.

13
00:00:42,194 --> 00:00:44,016
Nothing is half done.

14
00:00:44,066 --> 00:00:46,296
The payments product works the same way.

15
00:00:46,346 --> 00:00:52,248
When a payment reaches its end, such as paid or declined, its news is written to the outbox.

16
00:00:52,298 --> 00:00:55,896
The outbox is a tray inside the bank's own records.

17
00:00:55,946 --> 00:01:00,336
The news and the payment's final state are saved together, in one step.

18
00:01:00,386 --> 00:01:03,288
Either both are saved, or neither is.

19
00:01:03,338 --> 00:01:08,280
So there is never a paid payment without its news, and never news without a paid payment.

20
00:01:08,330 --> 00:01:10,152
Next comes the relay.

21
00:01:10,202 --> 00:01:12,192
The relay is the postman.

22
00:01:12,242 --> 00:01:18,072
It keeps collecting the oldest unsent news from the tray, and passes it to the bank's message stream.

23
00:01:18,122 --> 00:01:20,784
Several copies of the app may run at once.

24
00:01:20,834 --> 00:01:24,816
Each copy takes different letters, so no two pick the same one.

25
00:01:24,866 --> 00:01:27,888
Every piece of news is labelled with its payment.

26
00:01:27,938 --> 00:01:32,640
So all the news about one payment travels in one line, always in order.

27
00:01:32,690 --> 00:01:34,848
Paid can never overtake created.

28
00:01:34,898 --> 00:01:39,144
The relay marks a letter sent only after the stream confirms it has it.

29
00:01:39,194 --> 00:01:44,136
So consider a crash at the worst moment: after sending, but before marking.

30
00:01:44,186 --> 00:01:47,376
When the app starts again, it sends that letter once more.

31
00:01:47,426 --> 00:01:49,200
That is at-least-once delivery.

32
00:01:49,250 --> 00:01:52,992
A letter may arrive twice, but never zero times.

33
00:01:53,042 --> 00:01:57,432
To make repeats harmless, every piece of news carries its own id.

34
00:01:57,482 --> 00:02:01,368
A reader who sees an id twice simply drops the second one.

35
00:02:01,418 --> 00:02:03,792
One more rule keeps this simple.

36
00:02:03,842 --> 00:02:06,048
The bank's records stay the truth.

37
00:02:06,098 --> 00:02:08,544
The stream only carries the news about it.

38
00:02:08,594 --> 00:02:10,656
Now watch the visible behaviour.

39
00:02:10,706 --> 00:02:12,528
A buyer pays.

40
00:02:12,578 --> 00:02:15,168
In the merchant portal, the payment shows paid.

41
00:02:15,218 --> 00:02:21,912
Open it, and the webhooks section soon shows its first piece of news, on its way to the merchant's server.

42
00:02:21,962 --> 00:02:25,032
Now stop the bank's app at the exact moment a payment is paid.

43
00:02:25,082 --> 00:02:26,976
Start it again a minute later.

44
00:02:27,026 --> 00:02:30,384
The payment is still paid, and its news still goes out.

45
00:02:30,434 --> 00:02:32,256
Nothing is missing.

46
00:02:32,306 --> 00:02:36,384
On the merchant's server, a piece of news may now arrive twice.

47
00:02:36,434 --> 00:02:38,376
Both copies carry the same id.

48
00:02:38,426 --> 00:02:41,304
The merchant keeps the first and drops the second.

49
00:02:41,354 --> 00:02:43,176
What does this protect?

50
00:02:43,226 --> 00:02:48,024
It protects the merchant from waiting forever for news that was lost in a crash.

51
00:02:48,074 --> 00:02:53,328
It protects the merchant from shipping goods because of news about a payment that never happened.

52
00:02:53,378 --> 00:02:58,272
And it protects the order of events, so a merchant never sees refunded before paid.

53
00:02:58,322 --> 00:03:01,560
The buyer gets their goods because the shop always hears.

54
00:03:01,610 --> 00:03:05,880
The bank never has to explain news that its own records do not show.

55
00:03:05,930 --> 00:03:07,752
So back to the tray.

56
00:03:07,802 --> 00:03:10,176
Decide and post in one motion.

57
00:03:10,226 --> 00:03:13,512
The postman may knock twice, but never zero times.

58
00:03:13,562 --> 00:03:16,704
Next: delivering the news to the merchant's server.
